Each directory is the root of one machine, with only the files the collector reads.

Captured from the running system (podman, 2026-11-18):
  debian-13.0    etc/os-release, etc/debian_version   docker.io/library/debian:trixie-20250811-slim
  debian-13.7    etc/os-release, etc/debian_version   the same image after "apt-get dist-upgrade"
  debian-12.11   etc/os-release, etc/debian_version   docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-20250811-slim
  ubuntu-24.04   etc/os-release, etc/debian_version   docker.io/library/ubuntu:noble-20240423
  alpine-3.20    etc/os-release                       docker.io/library/alpine:3.20.0
  fedora-43      etc/os-release, proc/sys/kernel/osrelease   a Fedora 43 workstation

Written by hand:
  freebsd-14.3   etc/os-release follows the template in FreeBSD's libexec/rc/rc.d/os-release
                 (freebsd-src, releng/14.3), filled in for "freebsd-version -u" = 14.3-RELEASE-p7.
  proc/sys/kernel/osrelease in the Debian, Ubuntu and Alpine directories: a container shows its
                 host's kernel, so these hold a release in the form that distribution's kernel
                 packages use. The two trixie ones are the kernels that were really installed
                 in that container (linux-image-6.12.38+deb13-cloud-amd64 and its successor).
  empty          a machine with nothing to read.
